\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You know the way Ezekiel often brings back themes, ideas, even entire chapters worth of material, but more intensely the second time? In chapter 26, Tyre thought she understood her situation; in reality, she only understood the commercial opportunities inherent in the situation, and that only if the LORD did not act.\'a0 In chapter 28, the prince of Tyre claims all wisdom; and that wisdom is directly linked to prosperous trade. In chapter 27, the wealth and trade is presented largely neutrally, even gloriously, though it does not prevent a fall, and there are hints of darkness.\'a0 But here the darkness is made explicit, and is given as a reason for the fall.\
Try reading what the previous instance of Claude said about chapters 26 and 27, then try reading Ezekiel 28:1-19, looking carefully at the Hebrew. Trade is not just a repeated theme - it comes back in a way that demands we understand its implications, even as it interprets the earlier uses of that theme. For we too live in an empire built on trade. We too must hear the metaphors, the judgement, the dirge, the rhetoric, the warning of this passage. We too must let all the words Ezekiel was given by the LORD speak.
