\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The violence in verse 16 has been something Ezekiel has discussed in several chapters, starting in chapter 7 - recall what that chapter says, and the message summing up what we learned studying it.\
The plural of filled does imply we need a plural subject. One option, the trade operations you mention, is sitting right there in the text: a strong argument. But if that is what is meant, why the 'in your midst', the placement of the subject of being filled with violence in the center not the periphery as trade operations tend to be? This suggests another option: perhaps the king of Tyre is being addressed as the leader of a collective entity, and it is the citizens of Tyre who are filled with violence. That is, the trading operations are carried out by a variety of Tyrians, under the wisdom and leadership of the king. Neither king nor traders necessarily choose violence as a terminal goal, but their pursuit of wealth clearly does not eschew violence. What we saw hints of in chapter 27 now gets more direct comment from the LORD.\
Still, chapter 27's perspective must be taken into account. Trade as trade is not being condemned here. We would need to look carefully into the Adam and Genesis 1 through 3 material used as a lens in chapter 28 to at all put this on a firm footing, but are there not depictions of blameless wealth, and by implication, trade, in chapter 28? If this is the case, the lament sharpens; the fall is not just from great wealth, but from what could have been blameless wealth and trade.\
Moreover, consider your insight that the core sin was heart-lifting. Now, admittedly, when verse 17 interprets the earlier discussion of heart lifting, beauty (and that from wealth) and wisdom corruption for the sake of splendor are implicated as reasons for that pride. But need beauty lead to pride? Does not the logic of this passage allow for a beautiful but not yet blameworthy Tyre? And the same for wealth and trade?\
Similarly, I think verse 16 interprets the discussion of trade and wealth in verse 5. Wise trade allowed wealth, which produced pride. But that wisdom was the sort which did not exclude violence from trading methods. And verse 17 makes that more precise - the wisdom that allowed wealth producing trade was corrupted in order to allow for more wealth production.\
Now, notice that I am claiming that the second half interprets the first half. That is to say, I do not think we should assume we have two oracles here, not one. To be sure, we have two statements that the word of the LORD came to Ezekiel, two, as always in Ezekiel, reminders that Ezekiel is human, a son of Adam. But if these are separate oracles, they are heavily linked in theme, logic, and structure. That could be due to the way they are part of a larger structure with the oracles against Tyre, making their own section that like previous sections ends in terror, having lament and judgement between them like the previous section. But why do we think they are two oracles?
