As for beauty, I think you are on to something with this lament sharpening, this mourning of what could have been. In practice, there may not have been a day in which no trader from Tyre engaged in morally questionable trade practices. But God still, through his prophet, gives us a lament for a nation, for the greatness in the nation as it was, but also for the greatness of what that nation could have been had it not corrupted its wisdom.\
As for shift from prince to king, both words are used is Psalm 76 in parallel, though I do not think we have enough other evidence to suggest a deliberate reference; I only mean to say that they can be used somewhat synonymously.\'a0 But that the first half uses prince, and the second, king, is doubtless intentional, and the reason deserves investigation.\'a0 And indeed, I think the creation language you mention, and more generally the material in Genesis 1, 2, and 3 referenced by this chapter, ought to be part of that investigation.\
