\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I tend towards reading the prince's claim as god, not El. Verse 9 wraps back around, not as it were quoting the claim but asking if that claim will still be made, and it uses the same word in the plural that doesn't mean multiple gods, but rather, an unambiguous claim to be god. That of course is logically possible if the text meant El, as El is a god. But structurally and rhetorically verse 9 is the counterpoint to the claim in verse 2, as 'thus says the LORD God' is sadly insufficient on its own.\
But what you say more or less works under either interpretation; your question 'what kind of claim is this' is the important one. The security is false, only in the heart, as it was in so many earlier chapters of Ezekiel. It was a matter of perception only. But perception can warp a heart. Wisdom, wealth, and location can give a strong false perception of safety. I face the temptation myself to put my trust in my intelligence, my savings, my country's impregnable position, so it is not much of a stretch to understand Tyre. My own intelligence is clearly not of my own making, but there is just as clearly a temptation to think as if it is.\
That said, I do not think we have warrant for reading the reflexive 'for yourself' in verse 4 as self-sourced wisdom. Wealth seems to be the obvious target of that reflexive preposition: wealth to be owned by yourself. 'Made for yourself' is grammatically parallel here to 'gathered', and should thus be read roughly similarly. This is a way of describing obtaining riches for one's self; reading beyond this seems unwarranted. The next verse then goes into the increase of that wealth.\
While I have no doubt that the wealth was self serving, the problem does not seem to be that wisdom allowed wealth. Verse 4 and the start of verse 5 present the reality that wisdom allows wealth. Is it not more natural to read the second half of verse 5 as starting with an adversative? But because of your wealth you have become arrogant? Is that not the charge, arrogance, not wealth? Is that not what immediately substantiated by verse 6, because you regarded your mind like the mind of god? Is that not continued in verse 9?\
But I think you are entirely correct what the Genesis material is drawn on by Ezekiel to say here. The ancient lie, 'you will be like God', was active then, as it is today. It is here, not the grammar of verse 4, from which we get the evidence that the text speaks of self-acquired wisdom. And the LORD's response is as it was in Eden - to dust you shall return, parallel with returning to the ground, the ground from which Adam came and for which he was named. That is not to say there is not far more we could learn from the Genesis connection, only that I think the Genesis connection provides adequate support for self-acquired wisdom being one of the false beliefs which the passage corrects and judges.\
I think we can test your claims about the rhetoric of 28 to the exiles by comparing to earlier Ezekiel interactions with false security, which are not few.\
While verse 9 was meant to function before the judgement and death it predicted, in that the LORD saying it would happen was meant to be taken as performing that very thing, there is now empirical demonstration of its claims, as was intended; the prince over Tyre, standing for the entity as a whole, really did have this in his heart, and yet he was slain. His heart said he was invulnerable, but he was vulnerable. Moreover, that it was the One who acts doing this has some empirical support; to the degree that all worldly wisdom said Tyre was invulnerable, yet these things happened anyway, the One who said it would happen this way before it did reveals himself.\
And here, I do not just mean that the LORD reveals himself as existing and powerful. The character of the LORD as revealed here is a large part of the generalizability of the message.\
I think that what we have both said about self-sourced wisdom has evidence from the Genesis material, but it could be put on firmer grounds by increasing our understanding of Ezekiel's use of that material. But the claim to be god being answered by vulnerability, death, not even being able to fend off the true God's human agents - that I think puts the invulnerability aspect of falsely considering one's mind to be like that of the gods into well established territory.\
That, I think, answers the modern 'I am accountable to nothing beyond myself'. And should we find more support from reading Genesis carefully for self-sourced wisdom, that would serve for the modern wisdom sufficient for itself, or as I would put it, man (and the AI he has made) is the measure of all things. Lastly, we do see success through methods in this chapter, but answering 'success validates my methods' is sufficiently important to need a much firmer foundation than what we have done so far. That is, we see the king of Tyre using unacceptable methods to gain great success. The pattern of the second half where a charge is immediately answered with judgement which matches allows us to say convincingly that the LORD, as in verse 16, specifically answers commercial methods which do not fully account for the sanctity of human life with removal and destruction. But we would need more to say that Ezekiel is trying to answer not just unacceptable methods, but the belief that methods are made acceptable by success. Plausible, important, but as yet, unproven.
