\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Starting with the seal or signet, we may have a metaphor allowing verses 11-19 to interpret verses 1-10, albeit one mixed with other metaphors.\
Whether wisdom is a property of the seal or of the king of Tyre which the seal represents is unclear to me, but either way it interacts with the wisdom of verse 5. Similarly, the beauty of the seal interacts with chapter 27 verses 3, 4, and 11, and chapter 28 verse 7.\
While we have little to no evidence for a reference here, Jeremiah 22:24-30 may help us understand what it might mean to refer to a man as a seal. There, Coniah is only hypothetically a seal; counterfactually, if he was a seal, he would be removed and his security would be removed. Even if he represented God's authority by being king, the authority would be taken away, even as the kingship in fact would be taken away.\
The seal metaphor points in the same direction as the end of verse 13, the creation language you mentioned but may not have fully connected. The king of Tyre is a created being, not God. As a seal, his authority is derivative, referential. This interprets the word prince from verses 1-10. Like Adam, he was given kingly authority, bearing the image of God and a command to have dominion. Like Adam as expelled, and perhaps like Coniah was cast out and given into the hand of Babylon, the king of Tyre was cast down, exposed before the nations. \
While gold and gems appear in priestly contexts, they also appear in Genesis 2, and more recently, as depictions of wealth. Connecting Tyre's wealth to the source of precious metals and gems in God's creation may be the intent, though kingly functions may also be in scope. Moreover, it may be hard for us today to understand the flavor of beauty and majesty specific gems spoke of at the time.\
The cherub also presents difficulties, as you say, though I think you made a good start. I think the somewhat parallel passage in Isaiah 14:3-23 may help us understand the mountain of God reference, alongside the hubris, the considering himself to have a godlike mind, and the reference to the Genesis 3 wanting to be like God. This confirms what you were saying about heart-lifting, and allows us to further interpret verses 2 and 5. \
But I think the clearest and most important part of the cherub imagery is that the king of Tyre was placed or appointed by God. This further clarifies the earlier created and seal or signet language.\
What the king was appointed to, I think, should clarify what wisdom should have served. Here, the interplay between Adam made in the image of God, a kingly figure given dominion, and the seal imagery, I think points the way. Notice that there is splendor before sin in these metaphors. Like humanity, the king of Tyre was given not just authority from the hand of God, but beauty and splendor. As that authority, that beauty, and that splendor were from God, they were to point to God. But as in chapter 26 Tyre misunderstood the fall of Jerusalem, here the king of Tyre misunderstands his authority, beauty, and splendor; he does not take account of God. Wisdom genuinely does lead to wealth, especially in a mercantile kingdom. But as you started to say earlier, one must not forget God as the source of one's wisdom.\
Still, wisdom does fit a king to rule. Wisdom is not denied or objected to, only its corruption. Consider Solomon's request for wisdom to rule his people. Solomon there was acting as God's regent, well proportioned, full of wisdom. Though Solomon too erred in his means of getting wealth.\
So in the end, the king of Tyre is described as Adam. Created, sinless, given splendor, appointed to rule, then high-hearted, sinful, cast out, dead. Believing the lie, you shall not surely die may also be implicitly in the description, given the description of false security.
