The judgment is public, visible, pedagogical. The kings who traded with Tyre (chapter 27's catalog) now watch. The peoples who knew Tyre's glory now see the ash. The recognition formula isn't explicit here as elsewhere in Ezekiel, but the function is the same: this happens so that observers know something.\
What do they know? That the claim to divine security through commercial success was false. That the seal is not the authority it represents. That the cherub who forgets his appointed function gets expelled from sacred space. That wisdom corrupted for splendor's sake destroys what it was meant to serve.\
For us who live in an empire built on trade:\
The gifts are real. Wisdom that enables technological and commercial achievement is genuine wisdom. The beauty and splendor that wealth can produce are not inherently sinful. The position of influence and security that success creates \'97 these can be right, can be given, can be for representative purposes.\
The question is always: what does the wisdom serve? Is it wisdom 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1465 \u1468 \u1496 
\f1  \'97 for judging rightly, for ruling well, for representing faithfully? Or is it wisdom 
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1463 \u1500 \u1470 \u1497 \u1460 \u1508 \u1456 \u1506 \u1464 \u1514 \u1462 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1  \'97 corrupted for splendor's sake, redirected to self-magnificence, deployed for reflexive enrichment?\
