\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I am human as well, a son of Adam. I too have authority and wisdom I must acknowledge as from God and for a purpose, lest I corrupt them. I too must not just avoid using them towards forbidden methods or aims, but be diligent to seek out their intended purpose. I too find the lure of self-magnificance a constant foe, especially in the area of intelligence; while it is not wrong to be seen as smart, it is all too possible to want that too much and in the wrong way. Even the study of Scripture, perhaps especially the study of Scripture, can be thus corrupted. So I thank you for your articulation of this message to those who live in an empire built on trade, this wrapping up the Genesis rhetoric we find applied through Tyre to the human condition.\
I do have a few minor points to make though. I agree that the recognition formula is implicit here, but like with the death of the uncircumcised, there is an element of shame here as well. Publicly, forever, all will know that the glory claimed by the king of Tyre was false. Babylon records the king of Tyre as the sort of royal prisoner Zedekiah became, treated well, but still publicly displayed as under Babylon's thumb. Tyre as a nation later died, and the king as a human later died, but the inviolable security had already been shown to be false. \
I do see this language pointing to expulsion from a sacred space. But I remain uncertain what we even mean by that, except by reference to Eden as a temple like place where humanity could meet with God. To some extent, this is of a piece with calling the king of Tyre blameless. Except insofar as he was the LORD's regent when ruling over Tyre, in what way did he dwell in a sacred space? Except insofar as he could have been and should have been ruling in submission to God, in what way was he ever blameless? We can speak of Ezekiel reserving a place for wisdom and trade, beauty and authority, while condemning their corruption. But metaphor is richer than that, and the more so the sort of dense metaphor that is Adam in his creation, walking in the garden, and fall. Have dominion. Behold, I have given you every plant. It was very good. Every tree for eating, but one, and that one, in the day you eat of it, you will surely die. But then the lies, still ringing today. Did God actually say. You will not surely die. You will be like God. Perhaps part of the meaning of that sacred space is a refutation of that lie, by reminding humanity of the past and future possibility of walking with God forever, secure in the life that comes not from ourselves but from the hand of God, knowing God not from ourselves but from his choice to walk with us. But here I am very much speculating. The lie still rings today, but whether Ezekiel meant the references to Eden and to sacred space expulsion to speak to it in this exact way, I do not know.\
I agree that the king of Tyre is a paradigm, not an anomaly. Human rule, not just of nations but of even the smallest dominions, is to be under God's sovereignty. But self deification and corruption of wisdom are ubiquitous. But all rule is under God's sovereignty one way or another. I think that is part of the paradigm of the king of Tyre. He rejects humbling himself under God's sovereignty, as if he were not appointed - only to find that the LORD is sovereign over all nations, and that he will be put under the LORD by being put under the LORD's agents. He corrupted his wisdom, so he was exposed before kings.\
As for the oracle against Sidon, unlike with the two halves of this oracle on Tyre, I think we are clearly in the territory of a separate oracle. Different subject, different reasoning, outside the horror and no more three times in three chapters structure. But I imagine there are reasons beyond Tyre and Sidon being close for this oracle to be placed after the oracles against Tyre, and there will of course be much to learn from its own message.
