The restoration may be intimately connected to the LORD's glory and holiness. Tyre saw commercial opportunity in chapter 26, seeing the LORD as inactive or powerless rather than as revealing his holiness in judging his own people. The gods of other nations, if they did not protect their people, were seen as weak. God, having for a time given his people the blessing of revealing himself in judgement, may have appeared similarly weak. Indeed, to avoid this has been part of the reason for Ezekiel being told in advance the how, the why, and perhaps especially the when of that judgement. Judging other nations for their crimes is one way the LORD will show himself powerful, glorious, and holy. This is as it was when the LORD revealed himself to Egypt, getting glory on Pharaoh at the Red Sea. But that act at the Red Sea did not just humiliate Egypt; it also rescued the LORD's people. Showing not just the power to harm, but the power and disposition to rescue will reveal the LORD to his own people. So Tyre is not only blind, as in this chapter, to the LORD's gifts to their own nation. Tyre is blind to the LORD's acts on behalf of his own people, even the judgement against those people. And above all, Tyre is blind to the LORD's self revelation in those gifts and that judgement. But when the judgement falls upon themselves, then they will know.
