\b \cf2 What does Ezekiel 28 reveal about the LORD's character?
\b0 \
First, the LORD sees through positional security to the heart's actual claim. Verse 2: the prince sits in the heart of the seas, in the seat of gods \'97 but YHWH addresses what the heart says, not what the position appears to be. The commercial success, the island fortress, the naval power \'97 none of this obscures from YHWH what the heart has made itself. 
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1464 \u1468 \u1489 \u1463 \u1492 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1  \'97 your heart is lifted up. The diagnosis penetrates the appearance.\
This means: no framework succeeds in hiding its actual orientation from the One who judges. The metrics that seem to validate, the success that seems to prove, the position that seems secure \'97 these don't constitute the reality YHWH addresses. He addresses the heart.\
Second, the LORD names precisely. The accusation isn't vague ("you're bad") but specific: you claimed divine status, you made your heart like a god's heart, your wisdom was corrupted for splendor's sake, your trade filled your midst with violence. Each element identified. The judgment in verses 7-10 and 16-18 matches the sin with exactness \'97 cast from the holy mountain because you profaned your sanctuaries, fire from within because violence was deposited within, death of the uncircumcised because you claimed to be beyond death.\
This means: the LORD's judgment is not arbitrary or disproportionate. It is diagnosis followed by fitting consequence. The pattern we traced \'97 wisdom corrupted, violence accumulated, interior filled, fire brought out \'97 this is moral causation made visible by divine speech. YHWH doesn't impose alien punishment; he brings out what was put in.\
Third, the LORD gives before judging. The lament section establishes this. The king was created, placed in Eden, given beauty and wisdom, appointed as covering cherub, walked among stones of fire. 
