\f1  among the trade goods isn't invisible to Him.\
Any entity that claims immunity from judgment discovers that this LORD brings agents. The claim dissolves not in philosophical refutation but in historical subjection. The security proves false when tested. The divine pretension ends in the death it denied.\

\b Questions we raised but didn't finish
\b0 \
The question of whether Ezekiel addresses "success validates methods" \'97 I said we didn't have firm ground for this. But looking at the LORD's character as revealed here: YHWH judges the methods (violence, trade injustice) regardless of the success they produced. The success doesn't appear in the judgment as mitigating factor. The wisdom is acknowledged as real (verse 3), the beauty as genuine (verse 12), the wealth as actually achieved (verses 4-5) \'97 and none of this prevents or softens the judgment on how it was achieved and what it did to the heart.\
So while the text may not explicitly target the 
\i belief
\i0  that success validates methods, the LORD's character as revealed here falsifies that belief. Success doesn't validate. The LORD judges the violence that filled the midst regardless of the glory it produced. The LORD brings out fire from the interior regardless of how impressive the exterior became.\
The question of sacred space \'97 you noted uncertainty about what expulsion from sacred space means for Tyre specifically. I think we left this unresolved. If the king of Tyre was never literally in Eden, what does expulsion mean?\
Perhaps this: the king 
\i could have been
