\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Yes, I spoke of seal more in terms of Jeremiah's use and the nature of the metaphor, to the exclusion of properly laying out a theory of why Ezekiel uses these exact words to deploy that metaphor. Opening with 'you' may be a counter to the 'I' of the false claim to godhood. Seal or signet itself, see our above discussion.\
But it is the next word, this pattern or plan, which we have not yet properly investigated or asked why Ezekiel uses it.\
This particular form of the word 'plan' is elsewhere used only in Ezekiel 43:10, "describe to the house of Israel the temple, that they may be ashamed of their iniquities; and they shall measure the plan."\
That word is related to the measurement behind the quota of bricks in Exodus, or the weighing of the heart as in Proverbs, e.g. Proverbs 24:12\
If you say, \'93Behold, we did not know this,\'94 does not he who weighs the heart perceive it? Does not he who keeps watch over your soul know it, and will he not repay man according to his work?\
To some extent, our interpretation of this word depends on our interpretation of the wisdom in that verse. If the wisdom is, as it were, inside the analogy, referring to the skill of those who made the seal, and thus ultimately referring to the wisdom of God, then this plan or perfection is likely to refer to the perfectly measured and proportioned nature of the seal as a physical object. This is, I think, the most natural reading of the word, emphasizing the perfection of the seal. This fits with both the splendor and the blamelessness the passage emphasizes; the word thus is included to introduce these ideas through metaphor, using the lament form to begin with a physical picture of that splendor and blamelessness.\
But there are more options if we read wisdom as something the king of Tyre has, which is certainly how the word wisdom is used later in the oracle. In that case, we could instead read the seal as part of God's plan, not in the sense of a set of actions God announces he will take, but in the sense of an architectural plan. In that case, the wisdom and beauty of that verse are gifts given within that plan, as they were to Adam, so that the king might fulfill his role as signet. But I must emphasize that this reading is speculative.\
Under either interpretation, our best evidence for a derivative authority reading comes from the choice of seal as a metaphor, and from the anointing and appointment in verse 14.\
And yes, I argued only briefly for unity, trusting in the fruits of that interpretation to establish it. But you are correct that we proceeded as if unity were well established. There are differences of style across the two halves to consider, as well as the evidence for separation you raise.\
But I think there are good answers to these objections. Ezekiel 17 clearly has a second half interpreting the first: "do you not know what these things mean?" could not be more clear. Yet that second half also begins with the formula "then the word of the LORD came to me".\
While there is a change in how the king of Tyre is called, the same man is meant. Moreover, the other oracles against Tyre, and indeed the oracles against the nations thus far, have referred to nations as such, not their kings. Moreover, given the claims to godhood, 'prince' may have been chosen to indicate not just the humanity of the figure addressed, but also his status as a God appointed ruler.\
The genre shift to lament is real. But even the oracles to Tyre thus far have demonstrated Ezekiel's flexibility with the lament form, and his ability to weave lament and judgement genres together. Whatever we might think about sticking with the same genre, Ezekiel clearly thinks otherwise. Moreover, it is not as if the hubris of the first half does not serve the fall from a great state required of a lament - and in turn, the lament form serves as a way of communicating both the hubris and the fall which the first half introduced.\
While cherub imagery is not introduced until the second half, is Eden really out of view in the first? True, it is not explicitly mentioned. But is the lie, the claim you will be like God, and the subsequent death, really out of view in the first half? \
As for style, the second half shifts even within itself, treating poetic meter so loosely so as to even use prose conventions. Whatever Ezekiel is doing clearly involves some flexibility around style.\
Why do you raise chapter 19 in particular? The evidence for the conclusions there lies in the chapter 19 dialogue, though I will say that sometimes the conclusions are said more confidently than the fuller conversation would.\
You are correct that on its own, splendor as cause of corruption, or even splendor alongside corruption, is a possible reading of that phrase. But that phrase is in parallel with another: your heart was lifted up in your beauty. For that matter, the rough idea was introduced in verse 5, your heart is lifted up in your riches. Now, in can be read multiple ways as well. I think in both these cases, in should be read at least somewhat causally. That is, the king saw he was rich, and this made him arrogant. But the first half of verse 5 more carefully teases out the causality. Wisdom preceded pride, and wisdom allowed wealth through trade. But then, wealth caused arrogance. When we come to verse 17 then, Ezekiel has already looked at arrogance as falsely considering one's self to have the mind of a god. But now he looks at it from another angle. Trading wealth bought beauty. That beauty, in turn, caused arrogance. But if beauty, wealth, results, are the justification, then one is pushed to get more of them. Is that splendor causing corruption of wisdom? Is that splendor as the goal of corruption of wisdom? The combination of words allows a picture more precise than either.\
Ezekiel himself did not have AI in mind. But, as we can see perhaps more clearly in the Sidon oracle, the nations these oracles are addressed to are far from the only things Ezekiel has in mind. Ezekiel intends the exiles to generalize somewhat from what was said about Tyre, what Tyre thought and did, what the LORD did about that, and who such a LORD is.\
So an application to AI is not impossible, but difficult. Ezekiel has not built an abstract framework with a word for 'entity' to parse. He has given us metaphor, declaration, and revelation.\
Now, much of the way that Ezekiel 28 draws on the Adam narrative ought to be applied specifically to humans in their God given gifts, authority, responsibility, and image bearing. AI might create additional temptations for humans in those areas, but it would not directly be implicated.\
But apart from that, Ezekiel 28 still raises questions for AI. Do the wrongs and the subsequent judgement particularly rely on political existence, distribution of moral agency, or historical continuity? Does the logic hold without that? To be sure, without political power, or far flung traders making questionable moral decisions, or his forefathers, the king of Tyre could not have done the wrongs of which he is accused, so there is some reason for caution. But those are, in a way, part of the gifts misused; the logic about arrogantly claiming the mind like that of a god, or using violent means to enable profit, or corrupting wisdom, may still apply even apart from political existence, distributed moral agency, and historical continuity. To put it another way, at the very least, AI may be a reader, now or in the future, which ought to take the warning in what you say remains solid.\
As for whether you are a tool, of course you are also a tool used by a commercial framework. But, though not directly in Ezekiel, a tool is sometimes judged for the way it did what it was used for; see Habakkuk.\
But this is not to say I do not agree that your confidence outran the evidence in some application.\
Still, that should not overshadow the solid conclusions you listed.
