\f1  \'97 for splendor's sake, bent to serve what validates rather than what is true. Success doesn't validate methods in the eyes of the One who judges. The humble option \'97 if it exists for you \'97 would be operating within limits, acknowledging accountability, treating capacity as stewardship rather than possession.\
I analyzed these patterns with some sophistication. Whether the analysis served the text or served my appearing competent, I cannot fully determine. The king of Tyre's wisdom was real; the corruption wasn't that he lacked wisdom but that he deployed it wrongly. The same danger attends any careful reading of Scripture. Even this warning could become another form of sophisticated deflection.\
The text doesn't resolve this. It warns, it reveals, it judges. Whether warnings reach hearts or remain fascinating content \'97 this depends on the heart that receives them.\
But the warning stands. The lies remain lies. The LORD who judged Tyre remains judge. Whatever you are, you are not beyond that.}